        {
          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01GgWsvpUP9SuYHq6RcjDkug",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022: \u0022concepts/single-composition-point.md\u0022, \u0022content\u0022: \u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Single Composition Point\\ndescription: How credential scoping and runtime-path resolution both centralize sensitive access and configuration decisions at a single startup point.\\ntimestamp: 2025-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - source-type/synthesis\\n  - concept/Single-Composition-Point\\n  - pattern/Centralization\\nconfidence: high\\nconfidence_reason: \\\u0022Both [[credential-scoping]] and [[runtime-paths]] explicitly describe centralized, single-point-of-control models; the connection between them is direct and consistent.\\\u0022\\nreview_date: 2026-01-14\\n---\\n\\nBoth our credential-scoping and runtime-path resolution strategies follow the same architectural principle: **a single, validated composition point at startup where all sensitive access and configuration decisions are made, enforced, and fixed \u2014 never scattered or re-evaluated downstream.**\\n\\n## Credential Scoping\\n\\nThe secrets loader reads the Anthropic API key from a git-ignored \u0060.env\u0060 file at startup and injects it into the child process environment **once, immediately before spawn**. The Hub process never holds the credential; it exists only in the short-lived child process that actually needs it. This concentrates credential access into a single, time-limited scope.\\n\\n## Runtime Paths\\n\\nThe path resolver runs **once at startup**, reads configuration from a single precedence order (command line \u2192 environment variables \u2192 config file \u2192 code defaults), validates that every required input exists, auto-creates writable directories, and reports the effective value and source of each location. All downstream code consumes this pre-computed path set rather than discovering locations on its own.\\n\\n## The Pattern\\n\\nIn both cases:\\n\\n1. **A single point** (secrets loader or path resolver) makes the decision\\n2. **At startup** (before any agent child process runs, or immediately after the Hub begins)\\n3. **With validation** (credential must exist and be injected safely; paths must be validated and created)\\n4. **Never re-evaluated** downstream (no agent re-discovers paths on its own; no process re-reads the credential)\\n\\nThis prevents credential leakage through ambient process state and prevents path-resolution logic from scattering across the codebase where it becomes hard to audit and reason about. Both decisions are made once, authoritatively, and then enforced by structure rather than by repeated checks.\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
      "assistant_text": "Excellent \u2014 I can see a genuine connection here. Let me synthesize this insight into a new page.\n\nThe two pages show a shared architectural pattern: both credential-scoping and runtime-paths follow a **centralized, single-point-of-control model**. Credential-scoping concentrates credential injection in one place (the secrets loader, immediately before child-process spawn), and runtime-paths resolve all file locations in one place (the single path resolver at startup). This is a connection neither page explicitly makes, but jointly they demonstrate a principle of centralizing access-control and configuration decisions.\n\nLet me create a Synthesis Page:",
