      "assistant_text": "The index update was blocked by a safety check \u2014 likely due to a format constraint I\u0027m not seeing in the raw output. Let me answer your question based on what I found; the synthesis page itself was created successfully:\n\n---\n\n## Connection Between Credential-Scoping and Runtime-Path Decisions\n\nYour credential-scoping and runtime-path decisions follow the **same underlying architectural principle**: both centralize sensitive access and configuration decisions at a single, authoritatively-determined startup point, never allowing them to scatter or re-evaluate downstream.\n\n**Credential-scoping** ([[credential-scoping]]) concentrates API key injection into one place \u2014 the secrets loader reads the key from \u0060.env\u0060 at startup and injects it directly into the child agent\u0027s process environment, never touching the Hub\u0027s own environment. The credential exists only in the short-lived process that actually needs it.\n\n**Runtime-paths** ([[runtime-paths]]) resolve every file location in exactly one place \u2014 a single path resolver at startup reads configuration (command line \u2192 environment \u2192 config file \u2192 code defaults), validates that all required inputs exist, auto-creates writable directories, and reports the effective value. No other code re-discovers paths on its own.\n\nIn both cases, the decision is made once at startup, with validation, and then enforced structurally downstream rather than re-evaluated. This prevents credential leakage through ambient process state and prevents path logic from scattering across the codebase where it becomes hard to audit.\n\nI\u0027ve saved this connection as a new page, [[concepts/single-composition-point]], that states this relationship explicitly for future reference.",
